Jimmy_B wrote on Sat, 20 Dec 2003 22:31:20 GMT:
> Jody M Friese wrote:
[post quest advice?]

> At this stage, you basically want to make yourself as strong as you
> can before
[snip good advice about mmarkers]

> You'll want a t-shirt (you can get one polypiling normal armors with
> a little patience), gauntlets of dexterity, and a helm of brilliance
> (or a cornuthaum).  Enchant and fooproof all your armor as high as
> you can, wishing for magic markers as-needed. Spend leftover wishes
> on artifacts (which you may or may not succeed in getting), such as
> the Eyes of the Overworld and the Orb of Fate.

> Cast detect treasure on each level and loot any vaults you find, as
> well as Ludios. Then find a non-coaligned priest, donate for
> protection until you have no gold left, then (carefully) kill the
> priest. Take the gold and repeat on another, doing any coaligned
> priest last (and not killing it). Remember there's a priest of
> moloch in the valley of the dead.

> Finally, gather up all your potions, cancel everything that's not
> either full healing, gain level, or an ingredient which can be
> turned into one of those through alchemy. Bless them, and start
> polypiling, keeping anything that can be turned into FH or GL. Mix
> the potions, drink lots of FH, and your max HP will enter quadruple
> digits.

That's all a bit too thorough for my taste. The risk conntected with
the (lots of) extra time spent in the dungeon is not worth the
benefits in my opinion:

* [GoD are not very special; a wizard doesn't really need a [HoB.
* once naked AC is brought down to 1, the chances of getting less
  through donating get minimal--no need to scour the dungeon for this
* Quadruple HPs aren't that useful--once you have a good AC and
  3-400HP or so you are most likely to suffer an insta-death.

Also, your advice takes the player to Gehennom without potions of holy
water--that's NOT very advisable.

